# Apt pinning for image/common/apt/docker.sources: only the four package
# names below may actually be installed from Docker's official apt
# repository. Everything else from that origin is pinned to **-1**
# (apt_preferences(5): a negative priority means the version is never
# considered for installation, full stop, not merely "deprioritized") --
# cross-review finding: a low-but-positive priority (e.g. 1) still lets
# apt install a package that exists ONLY at that origin, which is exactly
# what would happen for docker-ce's own Recommends
# (docker-ce-rootless-extras, docker-buildx-plugin) if they were ever
# pulled in; -1 forecloses that regardless of what an install step
# Recommends. (The four packages actually installed additionally use
# `apt-get install --no-install-recommends` themselves, so those
# Recommends are never even requested in the first place -- see
# image/common/README.md's build-step order -- this pin is the second,
# independent line of defense in case that flag is ever dropped.)
#
# This is the "apt pinning so only those Docker packages come from that
# repo" mechanism the image build recipe requires -- Docker's repository is
# trusted for these four package names and nothing wider.
Package: *
Pin: origin "download.docker.com"
Pin-Priority: -1

Package: docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-compose-plugin
Pin: origin "download.docker.com"
Pin-Priority: 600
